  "cli_result_text": "Submitted. The root cause lives in `lib/ansible/galaxy/collection/__init__.py`:\n\n- `install_artifact()` builds `collection_tar._ansible_normalized_cache`, a private dict keyed by `m.name.removesuffix(os.path.sep)` for every tar member \u2014 a workaround for a CPython `tarfile` bug where directory members with trailing separators weren't reliably retrievable via `getmember()`.\n- `_extract_tar_dir(tar, dirname, b_dest)` strips the trailing separator from the requested `dirname` and looks it up in `tar._ansible_normalized_cache[dirname]`, raising `AnsibleError(\"Unable to extract ...\")` on `KeyError` \u2014 instead of using the standard `tar.getmember()` API.\n\nThis custom cache is a fragile private attribute bolted onto the stdlib `TarFile` object, duplicating logic that `getmember()` now handles correctly on current Python. The existing unit test `test/units/cli/galaxy/test_collection_extract_tar.py` mocks the cache directly rather than exercising a real `tarfile.TarFile`, so it doesn't catch cases where a real archive's member names don't line up with the cache's normalized keys (e.g., `concrete_artifact_manager.py`'s `_tarfile_extract` uses real `getmember()`/`extractfile()` elsewhere, showing the inconsistency between lookup paths).",
